// F188 (stage 8 step 3): `abs` was the first polymorphic builtin whose type rule came from its
// declarations rather than from a hand-written arm. Its four `builtin fn` lines were already
// there, documenting what the arm checked; the arm went and the resolver checked them instead.
//
// **The entry's "nothing in the runtime changed" expired on 2026-10-22, twice over, and this
// fixture is where both steps are visible.** F188 left `abs` dispatched by its builtin id,
// because the resolver deliberately does not record a `builtin fn` on `call_fn` -- a builtin has
// no body and `interpreter.c` consults `call_fn` first -- so the conversion really was
// type-checking only, which is what made the remaining conversions cheap. Then the four
// declarations became `native fn`, which retired the id, its branch and `call_abs`; and then the
// name left the prelude for the `math` module, so it is `math.abs(x)` behind a `use` and the
// implementation is `native_math_abs` in src/native_math.c.
//
// What survives all of that is F188's actual finding, which is why this fixture is still worth
// keeping: four declarations under one name, each a different scalar, resolved from the arguments
// with no arm anywhere. The spelling moved; the mechanism is the one F188 built.
use math

fn main() {
    print(math.abs(-3))
    print(math.abs(-2.5))
    print(math.abs(-7n))
    print(math.abs(-1.50d))
    print(math.abs(0))
}
